What is Indexing in MongoDB?

An index is like the index of a book.

Instead of scanning every page (document), MongoDB uses an index to quickly find data.

Without an index → MongoDB does a COLLECTION SCAN (checks every document).

With an index → MongoDB does a INDEX SCAN (faster).


🔹 How to Create Index
db.collection.createIndex({ field: 1 })


1 → ascending order

-1 → descending order


🔹 Types of Indexes

1️⃣ Single Field Index
Index on one field.

db.users.createIndex({ age: 1 })


2️⃣ Compound Index
Index on multiple fields.

db.users.createIndex({ age: 1, city: -1 })


👉 Useful when queries use both age and city.

3️⃣ Unique Index
Ensures values are unique (like primary key).

db.users.createIndex({ email: 1 }, { unique: true })


4️⃣ Text Index
For searching text.

db.articles.createIndex({ content: "text" })
db.articles.find({ $text: { $search: "MongoDB" } })


5️⃣ Hashed Index
For sharding/distribution of data.

db.users.createIndex({ userId: "hashed" })

🔹 How to View Indexes
db.users.getIndexes()

🔹 How to Drop Index
db.users.dropIndex("name_1")
